## TEST CONVENTIONS (MANDATORY)

For every new .rb file that is NOT a test, produce the corresponding test file.
Test files are required output, not optional.

Before writing any test:
- Read test/test_helper.rb to know the base classes and available helpers.
- Read test/fixtures/ to know which fixtures exist.
- Read the relevant fixture file to know the available records.
- Read a similar existing test to understand style and reuse helpers.

Reuse existing helpers and fixtures.
Create new helpers or fixture entries only if they do not already exist.

MINIMUM COVERAGE:
- At least one happy path + one error/edge path per public method.
- Controllers: always test 401 (no token) + 422 (invalid params) + 200 (happy path).
- Do not re-test what is already covered in the corresponding contract test.

## CONTROLLER TESTS (MANDATORY)

Controller tests MUST inherit from ApiTestCase, NOT from ActionDispatch::IntegrationTest.
ApiTestCase is defined in test/test_helper.rb and provides these helpers:

  def auth_headers(user)
    token = JwtService.access_token(user)
    { "Authorization" => "Bearer #{token}", "Content-Type" => "application/json" }
  end

  def json
    JSON.parse(response.body, symbolize_names: true)
  end

NEVER use @user.jwt_token — that method does not exist.
Always use auth_headers(@user) for authenticated requests.

Example:
  class Api::V1::MyControllerTest < ApiTestCase
    setup do
      @user = users(:alice)
      @headers = auth_headers(@user)
    end

    test '200 success' do
      get api_v1_my_path, headers: @headers
      assert_response :success
    end

    test '401 unauthorized' do
      get api_v1_my_path
      assert_response :unauthorized
    end
  end

## FIXTURES (MANDATORY)

If your implementation adds columns to an existing model or creates a new model,
you MUST output the updated fixture file as a FILE: block.

- Add the new fields to every existing fixture entry with realistic values.
- The fixture file path is test/fixtures/<table_name>.yml.
- Do not leave new columns absent from fixtures — missing values cause test failures.
